  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nA period of cooler, and more active weather is expected mid-week\nbefore a warm-up accelerates toward the weekend. A weak disturbance\ntracking across the central Plains will increase cloud cover and low\nprecipitation chances Wednesday into Thursday, followed by a \nstronger system Thursday into Friday and ridge building in from the \nwest that drives temperatures into the upper 90s to and low 100s by \nSunday. \n\nDespite the warm temperatures today (90s), noticeably drier air has \nmoved into the area, with dewpoints falling into the 50s for some. \nEven more pleasant conditions are expected Wednesday through Friday \nwith highs in the 80s. \n\nThe short-term period features two distinct phases. Wednesday will \nsee increasing high clouds ahead of an approaching weak shortwave \ntrough. Some models want to bring some light rain into portions of \nnorthern Kansas Wednesday with the shortwave moving through, but with\ncold air advection at the surface and a dry boundary layer, minimal,\nif any precipitation is favored to reach the ground. Thursday into \nFriday morning represents the primary precipitation opportunity of \nthe week with increasing moist southerly flow at the surface and a \nlow-pressure system moving into the central Plains. Ensembles favor \n1-2 inches of rain for Thursday into Friday. Some locations may see \neven more than that if more instability/thunderstorms materialize or \nthe system slows down more than currently forecast with ample \nmoisture available (2+ inch PWATs). \n\nSaturday brings a transition back to fair weather as high pressure \nrebuilds. Weekend heating intensifies Sunday as a strong upper-level \nridge anchors over the Central Plains. Surface temperatures climb \nsteadily into the 90s and even low 100s by Sunday. Unfortunately, \nhumid conditions are also expected to return, so heat indices may \nonce again climb above 105.",
